rowhammer (0.49.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New game mode "Hochwasser" (user request): Marathon under rising
    water. Every FLOOD_INTERVAL_MS of play time (20 seconds by default)
    a full row with a single gap is pushed in from below and the whole
    board moves up by one row; the round ends when the stack reaches the
    ceiling, and the row credit is the score. Everything else is
    Marathon - there is no goal to reach.
  * A flood row is a cell type of its own (instance id 0), so it can
    never take part in a gold or silver square, and pieces keep their
    identity and their square marking while they are lifted. A cell in
    the top row ends the round instead of being pushed off the board.
    The falling piece stays where it is unless the risen stack would
    swallow it, in which case it moves up with the board.
  * Own highscore list ~/.config/rowhammer/highscore-flood, ranked by
    rows like the Marathon list; every round is recorded, since this
    mode has no "did not finish" state. --reset highscore covers it, the
    "Highscores" and "Statistik" menu entries offer it as a fifth mode,
    and the statistics keep their per-mode counters for it.
  * The HUD shows the flood interval and the time until the next row in
    the two lines the timed modes use for their goal, with a label of
    their own ("Flut"/"Rest"); flood rows are drawn grey with a "::"
    glyph, in the color modes too.
  * Demo recordings gained their first event with a payload (the gap
    column, which is drawn from RANDOM and could not be replayed
    otherwise) and therefore format version 2; recordings of version 1
    are no longer accepted.
  * The manual's "Spielmodi" page was split in two, so the manual now
    has nine pages.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.48.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Demo recording no longer disturbs the round when the RAM disk it
    writes to, or the data directory it is finally stored in, runs out of
    space. Every write of the demo module is checked, and a failing one
    drops the recording with a note in the debug log while the round
    keeps running. The free space is deliberately not measured up front:
    such a check is only a snapshot of a directory shared with every
    other program on the machine, while the write itself is the
    authoritative answer, and a round costs about 2 kB per minute anyway.
  * The end of a round could put a truncated recording into the data
    directory: only the final move was checked, not the write before it,
    so a directory running out of space mid-header left a file that
    occupied one of the ten slots and pushed out an intact recording.
  * No message from the demo module can reach the terminal any more.
    Errors from its writes went to STDERR and were painted into the
    middle of the playfield, where the default render mode left them
    standing until something forced a full redraw; they now go to the
    debug log alone.
  * A recording that cannot be deleted no longer ends the round: the
    cleanup paths ran unchecked under "set -e".

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 12:30:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.48.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Multi-language user interface (roadmap item "Mehrsprachige
    Oberflaeche"): every text a player sees - the menus, the manual, the
    HUD labels, the result box over the board, the highscore and
    statistics tables, the wonder screen, the demo list, the reset
    dialog and the --help output - now comes from a translation table
    instead of being written into the code.
  * Two languages ship with it: German, the language the menus were
    written in and the reference the table is translated from, and
    English. A language is one file below lib/lang/ plus its entry in
    I18N_LANGS, so a third one needs no change anywhere else.
  * New option --lang de|en|auto (ROWHAMMER_LANG), also selectable in
    the settings menu and stored in the config file as LANGUAGE. The
    default "auto" takes the language from the locale variables (LC_ALL,
    LC_MESSAGES, LANG) and falls back to German when they name none of
    the supported ones. Switching in the settings menu applies at once,
    without restarting the game.
  * The config file is read and the language resolved before anything
    is printed, so --help and the --reset report already speak the
    chosen language. Diagnostics on STDERR stay English per the script
    conventions - they have to work when the language itself is what is
    broken.
  * A wrong option no longer dumps the whole usage text to STDERR but
    points at --help, which is the text that is now translated.
  * Fixed along the way: several German screen texts were longer than
    the 46 columns a 48-column terminal leaves and were cut off there -
    the multiplayer placeholder, the three rebind messages, the cancel
    footer of a confirmation and one line each on the first and third
    manual page. The width contract is documented in the language files
    now.

