rowhammer (0.47.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every all-time statistics counter now exists a second time per game
    mode (user request): cleared rows, bonus rows, gold and silver
    squares, rowhammers, pieces placed and play time are counted for
    Marathon, Ultra, Sprint and Time Attack separately, next to the
    rounds played per mode and, for the three timed modes, the runs that
    reached their goal.
  * The all-time counters are kept exactly as they were and are not
    summed from the per-mode ones. A round of an unknown mode - only
    reachable from a future mode - is counted in the totals and nowhere
    else, so the totals stay the complete picture and the per-mode
    screens show a gap instead of a wrong attribution.
  * The "Statistik" main menu entry therefore asks which set to show
    first, the way "Highscores" asks which list: the all-time counters
    on their three screens as before, or one mode's own counters on a
    screen of their own. The per-mode screen reads like the all-time one
    and adds the two figures that make modes comparable - the rows per
    round and, for the timed modes, the share of runs that reached the
    goal.
  * The per-mode data is stored as "mode_<mode>_<field>=N" lines in
    <data-dir>/stats. That replaces the "rounds_<mode>[_goal]" keys of
    0.42.0, whose two figures are now fields of the same scheme; an
    existing statistics file loses its per-mode round counts and keeps
    everything else.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.46.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rounds are recorded as demos and can be watched again from the new
    "Demos" main menu entry, which lists the recordings with date, mode,
    play time and rows and offers to play or to delete the one picked.
    The ten newest recordings are kept in <data-dir>/demos.
  * What is recorded are the moves, the gravity steps, the lock delay
    expiries and the piece stream of the round - not the screen. Playback
    feeds them back into the very game functions a live round uses, so a
    replay really plays the round again. That costs about 2 kB per minute
    of play and is independent of the terminal size, the colors and the
    render mode of either session; a frame recording would have pinned
    all of those down. The piece stream is stored literally rather than as
    an RNG seed, because RANDOM is seeded once per session, not per round,
    and its generator differs between bash versions.
  * While the round runs, the recording is written to a RAM disk only
    (XDG_RUNTIME_DIR resp. /dev/shm), buffered in blocks of 64 events, so
    playing costs neither frame time nor disk writes; the finished
    recording is moved into the data directory atomically when the round
    really ends.
  * Playback can be paused (pause key or space), stepped between 0.25x
    and 4x speed (left/right arrows) and restarted with "r" once it has
    finished; the quit key returns to the list. The clear animation
    scales with the speed, and the HUD shows the current speed.
  * A replay is never banked: no highscore entry, no wonder progress, no
    statistics and no recording of itself. It is refused while a round is
    suspended in the main menu, because it runs through the same game
    state and would discard that round.
  * Highscore entries carry a short hash of the round they were scored
    in, and the recording of such a round takes that hash into its file
    name. A recording that still backs a highscore entry is therefore
    recognizable as such and is kept when the demo directory is pruned,
    so the run behind a top ten place can always be watched again.
  * New setting --demo-record on|off (ROWHAMMER_DEMO_RECORD, default on),
    also in the settings menu and stored in the config file - unlike the
    render mode this is a matter of taste, not a property of the
    terminal. New reset target --reset demo for the recordings; --reset
    all covers them too.
  * The manual grew a page explaining the demos, with the number of
    recordings kept and the playback keys read from the live state.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 01:30:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.45.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * At the end of a round the game now asks for the name the round is
    filed under in its highscore list (user request). The player name
    from the settings is the default and comes preselected the way a
    graphical text field would be: Enter keeps it, the first character
    typed replaces it as a whole, an arrow key lifts the marking so it
    can be edited instead. The entered name applies to that one round;
    the setting - and with it the default of the next round - stays
    untouched.
  * The prompt only appears for a round that really enters a list, i.e.
    one with cleared rows, and for Ultra and Sprint only for a run that
    reached its goal. A round that is filed nowhere has no name to ask
    for; its rows still count toward wonder progress and statistics.
  * Both name prompts - the new one and the one in the settings menu -
    share one line editor that draws and reads the input itself. With
    that, the session-wide raw input mode has no exception left: the
    settings prompt used to hand line editing back to the terminal,
    which could not show a preselected default and echoed the typed
    name outside the centered menu block.
  * Only characters a name may consist of reach the input line, at most
    16 of them, so an invalid name can no longer be entered (and needs
    no error message afterwards).

