rowhammer (0.42.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New game mode "Time Attack" (user request): the round starts with one
    minute of play time running backwards and every row of credit scored
    adds a second back, so the run lasts exactly as long as it is kept
    fed. It ends when the clock hits zero - or earlier on a top-out - and
    the rows are the result. The HUD shows the play time bought so far
    ("Goal") and what is left of it ("Left") in the two lines Ultra and
    Sprint use for their goals.
  * The score of a Time Attack run is its rows, not the time it survived.
    The two are the same ordering - a run ending on the clock plays
    exactly the start time plus a second per row - and the rows are the
    game's scoring currency everywhere else, besides staying meaningful
    for a run the stack ended early, where that equation no longer holds.
    The list shows the play time next to them, because that is the column
    such a run is recognized by.
  * Own highscore list (highscore-timeattack, ranked by rows, top ten),
    covered by the "Highscores" mode picker and by --reset highscore.
    Unlike Ultra and Sprint it records every run, finished or topped out:
    this mode has no incomparable "did not finish" state - the rows are
    the same achievement either way, and a run that ended early simply
    has fewer of them, exactly like a Marathon round.
  * The statistics count the rounds played per game mode now, and for the
    three timed modes how many of them ended in that mode's regular
    ending rather than in a top-out (target reached, full time played,
    clock run down). They are shown on a third statistics screen; the
    first one is full and the counters do not fit beside it.
  * The manual grew a seventh screen: the fourth mode filled the
    "Spielmodi" page to its last line, so its closing paragraph about the
    highscore lists moved to a page of its own - the part that is about
    scoring rather than about how the modes are played.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 21:30:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.41.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The play screen can be pushed to the terminal in two ways now:
    --render-mode partial|full (ROWHAMMER_RENDER_MODE, default partial).
    "partial" is the incremental renderer of 0.22.0 - only the lines
    that really changed are rewritten - and stays the default because it
    is the cheaper mode by roughly half the time and a fourteenth of the
    output per frame. "full" rewrites the whole 48x22 block on every
    frame, the way the renderer worked before 0.22.0, as a compatibility
    fallback for terminals and multiplexers on which the incremental
    update draws incorrectly, and as a debugging aid when the frame log
    has to show whole frames.
  * Like --color-mode the switch is not a config file setting
    (precedence default < env < CLI): it is a property of the terminal
    in use rather than a matter of taste, and it has to stay reachable
    without editing a file when the screen output is the thing that is
    broken.
  * The screen is still cleared only when RENDER_FULL says so (menu,
    resize, round start), not once per frame: holding that flag at 1
    permanently would send an erase sequence with every frame and make
    the fallback mode flicker, the opposite of what it exists for.
  * The debug session header records the render mode (# render: in
    events.log), without which a frame log entry cannot be told apart as
    a whole frame or just the changed lines of one.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.40.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release structure on GitHub: a release is the tag "v<version>", and
    pushing it is the only thing a release takes. tools/release.sh is the
    one tool that knows about all three places the version lives
    (ROWHAMMER_VERSION in rowhammer.sh, the top stanza of
    debian/changelog and the Version tag plus %changelog of
    rowhammer.spec). It verifies that they agree and that both changelogs
    document the version, prints the release notes, and creates the
    annotated tag with those notes as its message. Pre-release tags such
    as v0.40.0-rc1 are rejected on purpose: rowhammer is a native Debian
    package, and a native package version cannot contain a hyphen.
  * The release notes are the changelog stanza for the version rather
    than a text of their own - the stanza has to be written for the
    Debian package anyway, and a second, independent release text would
    sooner or later contradict the package sitting next to it.
  * CI workflow (.github/workflows/ci.yml) on every push and pull
    request: bash syntax, ShellCheck, the ASCII rule, version
    consistency, the key-scan regression test (also with a byte gap, the
    issue #7 case) and both packages. The Debian package is installed and
    the installed game is run, because the starter is a relative symlink
    into /usr/share and a wrong path only shows after installing; the RPM
    is installed in a Fedora container, the only place where the spec's
    %files section - including the deliberately co-owned /usr/games - is
    really tested. ShellCheck gates at error severity, where the tree is
    clean; the remaining warnings are artefacts of sourcing lib/*.sh into
    the main script and are reported without failing the job.
  * Release workflow (.github/workflows/release.yml) on a v* tag: it
    checks that the tag name and the version in the tree agree, builds
    the .deb, the .rpm, the .src.rpm and a source tarball of the tagged
    commit, adds SHA256SUMS and publishes the GitHub release with those
    assets. It uses the runner's own gh CLI rather than a third-party
    action, and updates an existing release instead of failing, so an
    interrupted run can simply be repeated.
  * build-deb.sh and build-rpm.sh now print their status messages when
    the environment variable CI is set, not only on a terminal. A CI
    runner has neither a terminal nor a journal anybody reads, so both
    scripts used to fail there in complete silence, error messages
    included.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.39.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New game mode "Sprint" (user request): score as much row credit as
    possible within SPRINT_TIME_MS (3 minutes) of play time. The mirror
    image of Ultra - the time is fixed and the rows are the result -
    so it mirrors Ultra's decisions as well: weighted rows count (not
    physical lines), the HUD shows the goal and what is left of it in
    the same two left-pane slots ("Goal" = the limit, "Left" = the
    remaining time), and only a run that played its full time is
    recorded. The check sits in the game loop right behind
    play_clock_tick and before the tick's gravity, so no piece falls or
    locks on time that is already over.
  * Sprint runs are kept in a list of their own,
    <data-dir>/highscore-sprint (HSS_* in lib/highscore.sh): same line
    format and ranking as the Marathon list - the same number in the
    same unit - but a separate file, because a three minute run and a
    round that ends only on a top-out do not measure the same thing.
    highscore_sprint_screen shows it in the layout of the other two
    lists; where the Marathon screen has the play time, this one shows
    the physical lines cleared, since every entry ran the same three
    minutes. "Highscores" and --reset highscore cover the third list.
  * The Anleitung got a sixth page explaining the three game modes; it
    reads the Ultra target and the Sprint time limit from the live
    constants, like the wonder page reads its costs.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.38.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Show the Ultra highscore list (user request). 0.34.0 deliberately
    shipped its storage without a screen, so the only place a run's rank
    ever appeared was the round-end box. highscore_ultra_screen
    (lib/highscore.sh) now draws it in the layout of the Marathon list -
    two lines per entry, paged via menu_pages, same column widths and
    the same coloring - but ranked by the shortest play time, printed to
    the millisecond (fmt_duration_ms, MM:SS.mmm) and carrying the accent
    color on the time column: time is the score there, where the
    Marathon list has rows. The rows column stays next to it, since an
    Ultra run ends at or beyond the target and the overshoot is worth
    seeing.
  * Ask for the mode under the "Highscores" main menu entry
    (menu_highscores, lib/menu.sh): Marathon, Ultra or back. The two
    lists rank by different numbers and live in separate files, so one
    merged table would mix two orderings. The picker stays up until
    "Zurueck" or ESC, so comparing both lists needs no walk back through
    the main menu, and each screen's title names its mode.

 -- roemer2201 <r.oliver@web.de>  Sun, 02 Aug 2026 23:30:00 +0200

rowhammer (0.37.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add RPM packaging: rowhammer.spec in the repository root calls the same
    "make install DESTDIR=... PREFIX=/usr" that debian/rules calls, so the
    RPM installs the identical layout (/usr/share/rowhammer plus the
    /usr/games/rowhammer launcher symlink) and a layout change stays a
    Makefile-only edit. The package is noarch and requires bash >= 4.0,
    with ncurses recommended for the optional tput support.
  * Add build-rpm.sh as the counterpart to build-deb.sh: it builds the
    source tarball from the working tree, runs rpmbuild in a private
    _topdir below the output directory so the caller's ~/rpmbuild stays
    untouched, and collects the packages in dist/. It offers
    --output-dir, --release, --srpm, --keep-build, --verbose and --silent,
    each with a matching ROWHAMMER_RPM_* environment variable, and refuses
    to build when the spec's Version and ROWHAMMER_VERSION in rowhammer.sh
    disagree.

